**`ChatModel` / `ChatClient`**
- `ChatModel` is the low-level synchronous interface.
- `ChatClient` is the fluent, higher-level wrapper built on top of `ChatModel`. You'll use `ChatClient` in most application code.

**`Prompt` and `Message`**
- A `Prompt` wraps one or more `Message` objects (system message, user message, assistant message).
- `PromptTemplate` lets you define parameterized prompts in external files or strings and fill them at runtime.

**`EmbeddingModel`**
- Converts text to float vectors. Used for semantic search and RAG.

**`VectorStore`**
- Stores and queries embeddings. `SimpleVectorStore` is in-memory for testing; production uses Pinecone, Redis, or pgvector.

**Advisors**
- Intercept `ChatClient` calls. `QuestionAnswerAdvisor` wires in a `VectorStore` to do RAG automatically. `MessageChatMemoryAdvisor` maintains conversation history.

```java
@Service
public class AiAssistant {
    private final ChatClient chatClient;

    public AiAssistant(ChatClient.Builder builder) {
        this.chatClient = builder.build();
    }

    public String answer(String question) {
        return chatClient.prompt()
            .user(question)
            .call()
            .content();
    }
}
```

**Direct OpenAI SDK call:**
```java
OpenAIClient openai = new OpenAIClientBuilder()
    .credential(new AzureKeyCredential(apiKey))
    .buildClient();

ChatCompletionsOptions options = new ChatCompletionsOptions(
    List.of(new ChatRequestUserMessage(question))
);
ChatCompletions result = openai.getChatCompletions("gpt-4o", options);
String answer = result.getChoices().get(0).getMessage().getContent();
```
This code is OpenAI-specific, not injectable as a Spring bean, and has no Advisor slot.

**Spring AI ChatClient call:**
```java
@Service
public class AiService {
    private final ChatClient chatClient;

    public AiService(ChatClient.Builder builder) {
        this.chatClient = builder
            .defaultAdvisors(new MessageChatMemoryAdvisor(new InMemoryChatMemory()))
            .build();
    }

    public String answer(String question) {
        return chatClient.prompt()
            .user(question)
            .call()
            .content();
    }
}
```
Swap from OpenAI to Anthropic: change `spring-ai-openai-spring-boot-starter` to `spring-ai-anthropic-spring-boot-starter` and update the config key. The `AiService` class doesn't change.

**OpenAI (application.properties):**
```properties
spring.ai.openai.api-key=${OPENAI_API_KEY}
spring.ai.openai.chat.options.model=gpt-4o
spring.ai.openai.chat.options.temperature=0.7
spring.ai.openai.chat.options.max-tokens=1024
```

**Anthropic:**
```properties
spring.ai.anthropic.api-key=${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY}
spring.ai.anthropic.chat.options.model=claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022
spring.ai.anthropic.chat.options.max-tokens=1024
```

**Google Vertex AI Gemini:**
```properties
spring.ai.vertex.ai.gemini.project-id=${GCP_PROJECT_ID}
spring.ai.vertex.ai.gemini.location=us-central1
spring.ai.vertex.ai.gemini.chat.options.model=gemini-2.0-flash
```

**Injecting the auto-configured bean:**
```java
@Service
public class AiService {
    private final ChatModel chatModel;

    public AiService(ChatModel chatModel) {
        this.chatModel = chatModel;
    }

    public String ask(String question) {
        return chatModel.call(new Prompt(question)).getResult().getOutput().getContent();
    }
}
```

**Overriding options per call:**
```java
ChatResponse response = chatModel.call(
    new Prompt(
        "Explain Java generics",
        OpenAiChatOptions.builder()
            .withTemperature(0.3f)
            .withMaxTokens(512)
            .build()
    )
);
```

```java
@Configuration
public class AiConfig {

    @Bean
    @Primary
    public ChatClient primaryChatClient(
            @Qualifier("openAiChatModel") ChatModel openAiModel) {
        return ChatClient.builder(openAiModel).build();
    }

    @Bean
    public ChatClient fallbackChatClient(
            @Qualifier("anthropicChatModel") ChatModel anthropicModel) {
        return ChatClient.builder(anthropicModel).build();
    }
}
```
Your routing service can inject both `ChatClient` beans and decide which one to call based on load, cost, or capability.

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/ai")
public class AiStreamController {

    private final ChatClient chatClient;

    public AiStreamController(ChatClient.Builder builder) {
        this.chatClient = builder.build();
    }

    @GetMapping(value = "/stream", produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE)
    public Flux<String> streamAnswer(@RequestParam String question) {
        return chatClient.prompt()
            .user(question)
            .stream()
            .content();  // Flux<String> — just the token text per chunk
    }
}
```

If you need the full `ChatResponse` per chunk (for metadata like finish reason or token usage):
```java
@GetMapping(value = "/stream-full", produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE)
public Flux<ChatResponse> streamFull(@RequestParam String question) {
    return chatClient.prompt()
        .user(question)
        .stream()
        .chatResponse();  // Flux<ChatResponse>
}
```

**Low-level with ChatModel directly (using StreamingChatModel):**
```java
@Autowired
private StreamingChatModel streamingChatModel;

public Flux<ChatResponse> streamLowLevel(String question) {
    return streamingChatModel.stream(new Prompt(question));
}
```

```java
@GetMapping(value = "/stream-safe", produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE)
public Flux<String> streamSafe(@RequestParam String question) {
    return chatClient.prompt()
        .user(question)
        .stream()
        .content()
        .onErrorResume(ex -> {
            // Return an error message token instead of breaking the stream
            return Flux.just("[Error: " + ex.getMessage() + "]");
        })
        .doOnComplete(() -> log.info("Stream completed for: {}", question));
}
```

The stream completes naturally when the model sends the `[DONE]` signal — Spring AI handles the protocol details. You don't need to send a termination token manually.
